          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as true. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not alter the focal expression’s clear avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is framed by an invocation of Cordelia’s existing filial bond, giving it weak obligation-related force. It does not itself promise future conduct or create a new undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, measured assertion rather than a reflex-like discharge of emotion. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty giving outward verbal form to her heart.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The focal expression’s core force is adequately represented as an avowal, with a weak contextual invocation of filial obligation. No additional independent force is clearly carried by the marked words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say how much they love him while allocating portions of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, explicitly limiting it by her filial bond rather than competing in expansive praise.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia does not make a new promise in the focal expression. Her continuation invokes an existing daughterly bond and associated duties. Lear does not accept the answer as satisfactory; he asks her to amend it, warning that her fortunes are at risk.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied source identifies and contains William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear, Act I, Scene I.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence supports the avowal and shows Cordelia’s own claim that her love exceeds her speech, but it cannot independently establish the full history of the relationship or every practical term of the filial bond.\"}"
        }
